<Layout id="isa" title="ISA">

  <h1>ISA</h1>
<p>
    The most interesting part of the ISA is the
    <strong> Code Morphing Software (CMS)</strong>. CMS takes
    <strong> x86-compatible instructions</strong> and converts them into
    <strong> Very Long Instruction Word (VLIW)</strong> instructions that are
    compatible with the native hardware.
  </p>

  <p>
    The advantage of this approach is that the VLIW engine can be modified
    with only minor changes to the ISA. The processor can also remain
    compatible with newer versions of the x86 instruction set if the
    Code Morphing Software is updated.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Atoms</strong> are essentially single instructions in the source
    program, while a <strong>molecule</strong> is a combination of multiple
    instructions.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Code Morphing Software decides which atoms can be grouped together
    to form a <strong>VLIW instruction</strong> that can then be executed by
    the native hardware.
  </p>

  <p>
    The x86 instructions are read and interpreted in their original order,
    but once they are translated, independent operations can be scheduled
    and executed in parallel or in a different order when it is safe to do
    so.
  </p>

  <p>
    The Code Morphing Software supports different execution modes for
    x86-compatible instructions, including
    <strong> interpretation</strong>,
    <strong> interpretation with code generation</strong>, and
    <strong> optimization</strong>. The mode of execution is determined using
    instrumentation code. The Code Morphing Software also performs
    <strong> register renaming</strong> while translating the code.
  </p>

</Layout>
